/**
 * Exporting Camel programs as ONNX models. The export is a pass: a function `(f: F) => F!` that
 * the pipeline applies (`camel m.cml std::macro std::opt onnx::export out=m.onnx`, or
 * `f->onnx::export_model<out: 'm.onnx'>` in a program). A program that exports one of its own
 * functions while it runs calls the same function with an example of the arguments, which fixes
 * the shapes the graph is translated at (the second signature in `stdlib/signatures/onnx.cml`):
 * `export_model<out: path>(step, (model, batch))`.
 *
 * The module is the exporter, not a runtime: loading it and asking what it can lower needs neither
 * the ONNX runtime nor a GPU; only an export writes a file. A graph that cannot be translated --
 * a call that remains, an operator without an ONNX lowering, a dtype or shape the exporter cannot
 * name -- is reported as an error value of kind `ExportUnsupported` that says which node, and the
 * file system refusing the file is an `IOError`; neither is a fault.
 */

/// Lists the operator URIs the ONNX backend can lower at its default opset, in the order the
/// lowering table reports them. A capability report only: it reads the table compiled into the
/// module and never touches the file system or the ONNX runtime.
/// @example supported_operators()
export func supported_operators(): string[] pure

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Passes: functions the pipeline applies to the program
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Writes the model of the program's `main` (or of the one `export_model` call it makes) as an
/// ONNX file. `out` names the file, and overrides the path of the one `export_model` call the
/// program makes (exactly one call is allowed then). `dynamic_axes` leaves those axes of the
/// inputs unknown in the model; `target` names the runtime whose capability data the model is
/// checked against.
/// @fails error value `ExportUnsupported` when the graph cannot be translated or the target rejects a node; error value `IOError` when the file cannot be written
with <out: string = "", dynamic_axes: int[]? = null, target: string? = null>
export func export_model::<F>(f: F): F! uses fs when is_function::<F>()
